  "narrative": "The 1990s were a vibrant time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture. While the natural hair movement was gaining momentum in the US during the 1960s, it continued to influence the decade with its emphasis on embracing afro-textured hair. Hairstyles like the Jheri curl, popularized by African-American hairstylist Jheri Redding, were all the rage among Black Americans, offering a \"wash and wear\" style that was easier to care for than relaxers. Meanwhile, traditional styles such as cornrows and box braids remained staples in many African cultures and the diaspora, prized for their protective properties and versatility. Dreadlocks, also known as locs, began to gain popularity during this era, while afros continued to be a symbol of Black pride and identity. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, but it's clear that the 1990s were marked by a growing celebration of diverse hairstyles and hair textures, laying the groundwork for the even more vibrant natural hair movement of the 2000s.",
